        "h": "The lecturer's five cases, worked through the three questions",
        "body": "### Case 1 — MARIAM, 7, Cairo public school\n- **The story:** **\"STARING SPELLS\" 50-100 TIMES A DAY**, **5-10 SECONDS each**, **eyes roll up briefly**. **NO post-ictal confusion. NO injury or incontinence.** **Failing grades.** The family believes it is an **\"evil eye\" curse**; income is limited and **transport to clinic is difficult**.\n- **The tests:** **EEG 3 Hz SPIKE-WAVE. MRI NORMAL.**\n- **The three questions:** **YES** — paroxysmal, stereotyped, no syncope features, EEG confirms · **GENERALIZED, ABSENCE** — 3 Hz spike-wave, brief stare, no recovery confusion · **IDIOPATHIC (genetic)** — childhood onset, normal MRI, family history common.\n- **⚠️ DIAGNOSIS: CHILDHOOD ABSENCE EPILEPSY (CAE).** **1st line ETHOSUXIMIDE (Zarontin) 15-20 mg/kg/day OR SODIUM VALPROATE (Depakin) 15-20 mg/kg/day**; **2nd line LAMOTRIGINE if valproate is contraindicated — teratogenic concerns in reproductive-age females**.\n- **⚠️ CRITICAL: NEVER use CARBAMAZEPINE or PHENYTOIN in absence seizures — they WORSEN the seizures.**\n\n### Case 2 — AHMED, 22, factory worker in Alexandria\n- **The story:** **\"STRANGE EPISODES\" 2-3 times a week**. **AURA: A FOUL SMELL.** **LIP-SMACKING movements**, **loss of awareness**, **1-2 MINUTES**, **post-ictal confusion 5-10 min**. **Factory machinery is a job risk**; frequent seizures mean **job loss**.\n- **The tests:** **EEG LEFT TEMPORAL FOCAL SPIKES. MRI LEFT HIPPOCAMPAL ATROPHY.**\n- **The three questions:** **YES** — olfactory aura, automatisms, altered consciousness, post-ictal confusion · **FOCAL with IMPAIRED AWARENESS**, temporal lobe — likely **mesial TLE** on semiology · **SYMPTOMATIC** — left hippocampal atrophy suggests **MESIAL TEMPORAL SCLEROSIS**.\n- **⚠️ CLINICAL PEARL: mesial TLE with hippocampal atrophy is a SURGERY CANDIDATE — 70 % seizure-free post-op** (`ep-9`).\n\n### Case 3 — FATIMA, 28, divorcee\n- **The story:** **\"CONVULSIONS\" 2-5 times a week**, **sudden onset with drama**, **ASYNCHRONOUS THRASHING**, **5-15 MINUTES**, **SIDE-TO-SIDE HEAD SHAKING**, **NO cyanosis, NO incontinence**. **Recent stigmatised divorce**, family conflict, **multiple ED visits**, **\"seizure\" medications failing**.\n- **The tests:** **EEG COMPLETELY NORMAL. MRI NORMAL.**\n- **⚠️ DIAGNOSIS: PNES.** Every row of `ep-2`'s table is against a true seizure. **NOT \"faking\" — a functional neurology disorder linked to psychological distress, trauma and life stress. Real condition. Real suffering. Needs compassionate, non-judgmental care.**\n\n### Case 4 — YASIN, 16, secondary school student\n- **The story:** **\"MORNING JERKS\" for 6 months**, **UPON WAKING**, **brief arm jerks**, **NO loss of consciousness**, **worsened by SLEEP DEPRIVATION** — irregular sleep and late-night gaming. **One recent GTCS.** **Grandfather had seizures.**\n- **The tests:** **EEG POLYSPIKE-WAVE 3-4 Hz. MRI NORMAL.**\n- **⚠️ DIAGNOSIS: JUVENILE MYOCLONIC EPILEPSY.** **Hallmarks: age 12-18 · FEMALE > MALE 2:1 · morning myoclonic jerks · sleep-deprivation trigger · 30-50 % have generalized GTCS · polyspike-wave 3-4 Hz.**\n- **Treatment:** **1st line VALPROATE 15-25 mg/kg/day**; **2nd line LEVETIRACETAM**; **⚠️ NEVER carbamazepine or phenytoin**; **lifestyle: REGULAR SLEEP, NO ALCOHOL**; **usually LIFELONG therapy**. **⚠️ Women need CONTRACEPTION — valproate is teratogenic**, and the deck adds: *consider levetiracetam if he were female*.\n\n### Case 5 — BABY OMAR, day 3 of life\n- **The story:** **\"JERKY MOVEMENTS\"**, **eye deviation and staring**, **multiple episodes an hour**, **post-ictal DEEP SLEEP**, **TEMPERATURE 38.5 °C**, **feeding difficulty**. **No prenatal care**, **difficult vacuum delivery**, **intrapartum fever**, **no antibiotic prophylaxis**. **CSF: PLEOCYTOSIS.**\n- **⚠️ THE RULE: NEONATAL SEIZURES ARE PROVOKED UNTIL PROVEN OTHERWISE.** **Find the cause → treat the cause → the seizures often resolve.**\n- **The neonatal differential:** **INFECTION (40 %)** — meningitis, encephalitis, **and Omar's fever and CSF pleocytosis point there** · **HYPOXIA-ISCHAEMIA** — birth asphyxia, difficult delivery · **METABOLIC** — hypoglycaemia, hyponatraemia, hypocalcaemia · **INTRACRANIAL** — intraventricular haemorrhage, stroke · **DRUG WITHDRAWAL** — maternal opioids, benzodiazepines · **GENETIC** — benign neonatal epilepsy, rare and familial.\n\n### The five on one line each\n\n|**Case**|**Diagnosis**|**Type**|**Key feature**|\n|---|---|---|---|\n|**Mariam**|**Childhood absence**|**Generalized**|**3 Hz spike-wave**|\n|**Ahmed**|**Mesial TLE**|**Focal, impaired**|**hippocampal atrophy**|\n|**Fatima**|**PNES**|**Non-epileptic**|**normal EEG, psychological**|\n|**Yasin**|**JME**|**Generalized myoclonic**|**morning jerks, polyspike**|\n|**Omar**|**Neonatal seizures**|**PROVOKED**|**meningitis**|\n\nSrc: L4 sl. CASE 1: MARIAM · CASE 1: DIAGNOSTIC REASONING · CASE 2: AHMED · CASE 2: DIAGNOSTIC & MANAGEMENT REASONING · CASE 3: FATIMA · CASE 3: WHEN IS IT NOT A SEIZURE? · CASE 4: YASIN · CASE 4: JUVENILE MYOCLONIC EPILEPSY · CASE 5: BABY OMAR · CASE 5: NEONATAL SEIZURES · BRINGING IT TOGETHER",
        "qs": []
