        "id": "ax-9",
        "w": "must",
        "h": "The drugs, hospitalisation and ECT",
        "body": "- **⚠️ *Antidepressant* is a MISNOMER**, and the book gives it a note box: these drugs treat **conditions other than depression**, and **anxiety disorders are the example**. A patient who reads the packet needs telling.\n\n|**Drug or class**|**Agents printed**|**Used for**|**Side effects printed**|\n|---|---|---|---|\n|**SSRIs**|**fluoxetine · paroxetine · sertraline · fluvoxamine · citalopram · escitalopram**|**ALL types of anxiety**|**nausea · headache · initial irritability · insomnia or somnolence · sexual side effects**|\n|**TCA**|**clomipramine**|**OCD** — worked in `ps-ocd`'s `oc-8`|—|\n|**SNRI**|**venlafaxine**|—|—|\n|**Trazodone**|trazodone|**insomnia and initial irritability AT THE START OF SSRIs**|—|\n|**Benzodiazepines** *(minor tranquillisers)*|—|—|**⚠️ NOT MORE THAN 1 MONTH**; the deck adds **short duration, NOT PREFERRED**|\n|**Propranolol**|propranolol|**social anxiety in DEFINITE situations** (e.g. exams), **ONE HOUR BEFORE**|**⚠️ CONTRAINDICATED in bronchial asthma**|\n\n- **⚠️ Trazodone:** it is prescribed **against the SSRI, not against the anxiety**. Two of the five printed SSRI side effects are **insomnia and initial irritability**, and trazodone is the book's answer to exactly those two at initiation. **The chapter's only drug-for-a-drug.**\n\n### ⚠️⚠️ NO DOSE, NO UNIT, NO ROUTE IS PRINTED FOR ANY DRUG\n- **All of it is timing:** the chapter's entire quantitative content is **four TIMING figures** — benzodiazepines **not more than 1 month** · propranolol **one hour before** · treatment **12 months** · onset **2-8 weeks (4-12 for OCD)**.\n- **⚠️ `L8`'s dose table:** it **must NOT be carried across**. The OCD deck prints target doses — fluoxetine 40-80, fluvoxamine 100-300, sertraline 100-200, paroxetine 40-60, clomipramine 100-250 mg/day — but those are **high-dose OCD targets**, which is the whole point of `oc-8`'s step 2. **They are not the doses for GAD, panic or the phobias.**\n\n|**Supplied dosing**|**Typical adult range** *(not taken from the course material)*|\n|---|---|\n|**Sertraline**|start **25-50 mg/day**, usual **50-200**|\n|**Escitalopram**|start **5-10 mg/day**, usual **10-20**|\n|**Paroxetine · fluoxetine**|start **10-20 mg/day**, usual **20-50** and **20-60**|\n|**Venlafaxine XR**|start **37.5-75 mg/day**, usual **75-225**|\n|**Trazodone**|**25-100 mg at night** for the initiation insomnia|\n|**Propranolol**|**10-40 mg**, one hour before the situation|\n\n- **⚠️ Start low in PANIC DISORDER specifically** *(supplied)* — the initial jitteriness of an SSRI is read by the patient as an attack, which is `ax-5`'s catastrophic misinterpretation, and it is the commonest reason a panic patient stops the drug in week one.\n\n### Hospitalisation — usually not needed, and four exceptions\n- **1. Suicidal trials** · **2. severe panic attacks** · **3. observation and investigation of the differential** (e.g. substance use disorder) · **4. comorbid conditions** (e.g. severe comorbid depression). **The deck agrees: usually, no need.**\n\n### ⚠️ ECT — and the two sources do not say the same thing\n- **The deck:** **ECT is NOT EFFECTIVE.**\n- **The book:** the response of anxiety and related disorders to ECT is **generally POOR** — **however**, ECT **can be used** for **suicidal trials** or **severe comorbid depression**.\n- **⚠️ Both recorded, neither corrected:** they agree ECT does not treat the anxiety, and the book's two exceptions are **two of the four admission reasons above** — it is being used for the comorbidity.\n\nSrc: neuropsychiatry.pdf (ASM MINDS) Psychiatry ch.3 printed p.201 — the misnomer note box, the drug list, Hospitalization and ECT; L4,5 sl. Treatment for the deck's ECT, benzodiazepine and hospitalisation lines; the OCD target doses are L8's, cited only to keep them out. Dosing rows marked supplied follow `CLAUDE.md` §4",
