        "h": "What the word means, what it does not, and who gets it",
        "body": "### ⚠️ Terminology — and the misconception the book opens by killing\n- **The Greek:** **skhizein**, *to split*, and **phrēn**, *mind*.\n- **⚠️ It does NOT mean split personality**, and it is **not multiple personality disorder** — the book says so explicitly, and calls the confusion a matter of **public perception**. What is split is **mental FUNCTIONS**, which is what the presentation shows.\n- **Japan renamed it in 2002:** from schizophrenia to **integration disorder**, **to reduce stigma**.\n\n### Prevalence and epidemiology\n|**Item**|**Figure**|\n|---|---|\n|**Lifetime risk**|**about 1 %**|\n|**Where**|**adulthood, at similar rates WORLDWIDE**|\n|**Onset**|**late adolescence and young adulthood**|\n|**Sex**|**slightly more common in MALES**, with an **earlier age of onset**|\n|**⚠️ Lifetime substance use disorder**|**almost 50 %**|\n|**⚠️ Life expectancy**|**reduced by 10 to 25 years**|\n\n- **Comorbidity:** patients are likely to carry additional conditions — **major depression** and **anxiety disorders** — on top of the substance figure above.\n- **Why life expectancy falls:** the book gives two reasons — **increased physical health problems** and a **higher suicide rate**.\n- **Social impact, three words:** **long-term unemployment** · **poverty** · **homelessness**.\n\nSrc: neuropsychiatry.pdf (ASM MINDS) Psychiatry ch.7 printed p.228 — Terminology, Prevalence & epidemiology",
        "qs": []
