        "id": "sz-5",
        "w": "must",
        "h": "The symptoms the lecture teaches — first rank, delusions, hallucinations",
        "body": "### Schneiderian first-rank symptoms — the deck's opening slide\n- **Thought insertion** · **thought withdrawal** · **thought broadcasting** · **delusions of control / passivity** · **delusional perception**.\n- **⚠️ Where they still bind:** DSM-5 dropped first-rank symptoms as a shortcut to diagnosis **except in one place**, and that place is the note box in `sz-6`. **Read the two together.**\n- **⚠️ Defined in full elsewhere:** thought insertion, withdrawal and broadcasting, and delusions of reference, are worked in **`ps-symptom`'s `sy-6`**, which was written knowing they would be needed here. **Not repeated.**\n\n### Delusions\n- **Definition:** **fixed false beliefs** that a person **strongly believes even when there is clear evidence that they are not true**.\n|**Type**|**The deck's example**|\n|---|---|\n|**Persecutory**|believing **strangers are spying on you** without any evidence|\n|**Grandiose**|believing you have **special powers or an important mission**|\n|**Erotomanic**|believing **a famous person is secretly in love with you**|\n\n### Hallucinations\n- **Definition:** **perceptions that occur WITHOUT an external stimulus** — the person experiences something through the senses **although nothing is there**.\n- **Five modalities named:** **visual · auditory · olfactory · gustatory · tactile.**\n- **⚠️ Auditory hallucinations — four forms:** the deck names them because **they are what the criteria turn on** —\n|**Form**|**What is heard**|\n|---|---|\n|**Third-person**|voices **discussing** the person — *She is walking to school now*|\n|**Running commentary**|a voice **continuously comments** on the person's actions|\n|**Command**|voices **instruct** the person to do something|\n|**Voices arguing**|**two or more voices talking about** the person|\n\n- **⚠️ Three of four are shortcuts:** to criterion A — **running commentary** and **two or more voices conversing** are named in the note box in `sz-6`; **third-person** is the form the note box is describing. **Command hallucinations are NOT a shortcut** — they are a **risk-assessment** finding, and they are what put the first case in `ps-schizmgmt` in the middle of traffic.\n- **Illusion against hallucination**, and the hypnagogic/hypnopompic pair, are in **`ps-symptom`'s `sy-7`**.\n\nSrc: L11,12 sl. Schneiderian first-rank symptoms · Delusions · Hallucinations · Auditory Hallucinations in Schizophrenia. The DSM-5 cross-reference is to printed p.232 of neuropsychiatry.pdf (ASM MINDS) Psychiatry ch.7",
