        "h": "The five subtypes DSM-5 deleted, and catatonia",
        "body": "- **The deck's own caption:** the subtypes are **no longer in DSM-5, but still clinically discussed**.\n- **⚠️ All five named, none described:** `Paranoid`, `Catatonic` and `Catatonia / How to examine for catatonia?` are **slide titles with a YouTube link and no text**; `Disorganized`, `Undifferentiated` and `Residual` appear only in the list. The descriptions below are **supplied — not taken from the course material** — and are given because the names are unusable without them.\n\n|**Subtype**|**Its defining feature** *(supplied)*|\n|---|---|\n|**Paranoid**|**delusions and auditory hallucinations** dominate; **affect, speech and cognition relatively preserved** — the best-functioning subtype|\n|**Disorganized** *(hebephrenic)*|**disorganized speech and behaviour** with **flat or inappropriate affect**; **early onset, poor prognosis**|\n|**Catatonic**|**psychomotor disturbance** dominates — see the row below|\n|**Undifferentiated**|criterion A is met but **the picture fits none of the three above**|\n|**Residual**|**no prominent positive symptoms**; **attenuated or negative symptoms persist** — the phase-4 picture in `sz-4`|\n\n- **⚠️ Two names are ALSO phase names:** and they are not the same thing. **Residual** is a subtype in this list and a **phase** in `sz-4`; **catatonic** is a subtype here and a **criterion A item** in `sz-6`. **Read the label against its context.**\n\n### Catatonia — where it is actually written\n- **⚠️ Catatonia — two slides, no words:** both are video links. **`ps-symptom`'s `sy-4` carries catatonia in full**, written from `L4,5`, and is the place to read it. **Not repeated here.**\n- **Why it still matters in this chapter:** catatonic behaviour is **criterion A item 4**, and **catatonia is the first indication for ECT** in `ps-schizmgmt`. **Three chapters touch it and only one defines it.**\n\nSrc: L11,12 sl. Schizophrenia Subtypes (no longer in DSM-5, but still clinically discussed) · Paranoid schizophrenia · Catatonic Schizophrenia · Catatonia — the last three carrying video links and no body text. The subtype descriptions are supplied per `CLAUDE.md` §4; catatonia itself is written in `ps-symptom`'s `sy-4`",
