        "w": "must",
        "h": "Differential diagnosis",
        "body": "### Other psychiatric illness\n|**Feature that misleads**|**What else it can be**|\n|---|---|\n|**psychotic symptoms**|**bipolar disorder** · **borderline personality disorder**|\n|**delusions that are NON-BIZARRE**|**delusional disorder**|\n|**social withdrawal**|**social anxiety disorder** · **avoidant personality disorder** · **schizotypal personality disorder**|\n|**obsessions mistaken for delusions**|**OCD** — which **occurs with schizophrenia more often than chance**, so it is as often a comorbidity as a differential|\n\n- **Schizotypal symptoms are similar — but LESS SEVERE** — the book's own discriminator, and the only one it gives for a personality disorder.\n- **⚠️ Obsessions against delusions:** printed as **DIFFICULT**, not as a clean test. The discriminator that works is **`ps-ocd`'s `oc-6`: an obsession is EGO-DYSTONIC**, a delusion ego-syntonic. **The book does not supply it here; the sibling chapter does.**\n\n### Other medical and neurological illness\n- **Eleven, printed as a bare list:** metabolic disturbance · systemic infection · **syphilis** · **HIV infection** · **epilepsy** · brain lesions · **stroke** · **multiple sclerosis** · **hyperthyroidism** · **hypothyroidism** · **dementias** (e.g. Alzheimer's).\n- **⚠️ Not one of the eleven is expanded.** No discriminating feature, no test, no clue. **The list is the whole content**, which is why `sm-3`'s *exclude organicity* has to be done on history and examination rather than on a panel.\n\n### Delirium — the one exclusion given real features\n|**Feature**|**Points to delirium**|\n|---|---|\n|**hallucinations**|**VISUAL**|\n|**onset**|**ACUTE**|\n|**consciousness**|**FLUCTUATING level**|\n\n- **⚠️ It indicates something else:** an **underlying MEDICAL illness** — which is the reason it sits outside the eleven-item list. **`nr-delirium` is where it is taught.**\n- **The modality decides:** **auditory is the schizophrenia default** (`sz-5`); **visual is the organic alarm**. **One word decides which way a viva goes.**\n\n### Substance abuse\n- **Drug intoxication** and **drug-induced psychosis** — the book's example is **AMPHETAMINES**, which is the differential the dopamine hypothesis in `sz-3` predicts.\n\nSrc: neuropsychiatry.pdf (ASM MINDS) Psychiatry ch.7 printed p.235 — Differential diagnosis, all four sub-headings as printed",
