        "w": "must",
        "h": "Hospitalisation",
        "body": "- **It may be VOLUNTARY or — if mental health legislation allows, INVOLUNTARY**, and it is for **severe episodes**.\n- **⚠️ Long-term hospitalisation is UNCOMMON** — since **deinstitutionalisation beginning in the 1950s**. The date is printed; it is the chapter's only historical anchor.\n\n|**Indication**|**What it looks like**|\n|---|---|\n|**1. Initial assessment**|—|\n|**2. Potential hazard of DANGER**|**to himself** — **suicide** and **excitement**; **to others** — **aggression** or **homicide**|\n|**3. Possible DETERIORATION of health**|**dehydration** · **total refusal of food** · **no insight and refusal of medications**|\n|**4. Emergency SIDE EFFECT**|the example given is **NEUROLEPTIC MALIGNANT SYNDROME**|\n\n- **✅ The deck — and the book print the same four indications**, in the same order, with the same examples. **The only clean agreement between the two sources in this chapter.**\n- **⚠️⚠️ NMS — named twice, described never:** here as a hospitalisation indication, and again in `sm-7` as **the** rare-but-severe adverse effect. **No features, no triggers, no management anywhere in the eleven pages.** The chapter defers it in place — ***(refer to psychiatric emergency)*** — i.e. to **Psychiatry ch.10, printed 256-260**.\n- **⚠️⚠️ CORRECTED 2026-08-23: that is NOT a reading debt.** This was first recorded as *unread, book only*. **It is taught in decks already cached** — **`L15` teaches NMS in full** (tetrad, **mortality 10-20 %**, **incidence 0.02-3 %**, **male:female 2:1**, **age not a risk factor**, D2 antagonism at hypothalamus and nigrostriatum, the associated medications, seven lab findings and a nine-step treatment), and **`L16` teaches it again.** **The gap was declared from the book’s own pointer without probing the decks.** It is **`ps-emerg`'s to write, from those two decks.**\n- **⚠️ Indication 3 is the compliance failure — and indication 4 is the treatment failure.** Between them they say the two commonest reasons a stable schizophrenia patient is admitted are **stopping the drug** and **the drug itself**.\n\nSrc: neuropsychiatry.pdf (ASM MINDS) Psychiatry ch.7 printed p.235 — Treatment: Hospitalization, its Indications and the poster restating them; L11,12 sl. Hospitalization, which prints the identical four",
