        "h": "Resistance, depot, psychotherapy and ECT",
        "body": "### Treatment-resistant cases\n- **CLOZAPINE** is effective for those who **respond poorly to other drugs** — *treatment-resistant* or *refractory* schizophrenia.\n- **⚠️ Its price is AGRANULOCYTOSIS** — an **uncommon but potentially serious** lowered white cell count. *(The chapter names the risk and prints no monitoring — a weekly-then-monthly full blood count is the standard answer, supplied, and `sm-3` records that the book never says it.)*\n\n### Non-compliant patients\n- **LONG-ACTING DEPOT preparations**, for people **unwilling or unable to take medication regularly**.\n- **The evidence:** they **reduce the risk of relapse MORE than oral medications**, and **combined with psychosocial interventions they may improve long-term adherence**.\n- **⚠️ Depot answers compliance:** **not resistance** — the fourth case in `sm-9` is a patient who was moved to depot for exactly that reason and **still** did not respond, which is what turns him into a clozapine candidate.\n\n### Psychotherapy — seven modalities named, none described\n- **Psychosocial interventions:** **family therapy** · **milieu therapy** · **supported employment** · **skills training** · **token economic interventions** · **substance use** · **weight management**.\n- **⚠️ A LIST IS NOT COVERAGE** — not one of the seven is described anywhere in the chapter.\n- **⚠️⚠️ AND THE TWO SOURCES DISAGREE ABOUT CBT.** The **book** prints a note: **evidence for CBT in reducing symptoms or preventing relapse is MINIMAL**, and **art/drama therapy are not well researched**. The **deck** lists **cognitive behavioural therapy** and **cognitive remediation** among the psychotherapies for schizophrenia without qualification. **Both recorded, neither corrected.** ⚠️ **This is the opposite of the anxiety picture**, where `ps-anxiety`'s `ax-8` has the deck naming CBT and the book naming four other methods — **here the book names CBT specifically to doubt it.**\n\n### ECT\n- **Indications, and the two sources agree:** **suicidal or homicidal attempts** · **CATATONIC STUPOR** · **EXCITEMENT** · **resistant cases**. The deck prints the same five as *catatonia, suicide, homicide, excitement, resistant to medication*.\n- **⚠️ ECT is a ONE-LINE section — indications only.** **No technique, no course length, no electrode placement, no anaesthesia, no contraindication and no adverse effect**, and the chapter prints **no pointer** to where the rest lives. **`ps-devices` is the obvious home** — book Psychiatry ch.12, printed 265-270, **not read**.\n- **⚠️ Catatonia is ECT's first indication — and this chapter never defines it.** `ps-symptom`'s `sy-4` does.\n\nSrc: neuropsychiatry.pdf (ASM MINDS) Psychiatry ch.7 printed p.238 — Treatment of resistant cases & non-compliant patients, Psychotherapy with its CBT note, ECT, and the vertical flow poster; L11,12 sl. Electroconvulsive therapy · Psychotherapy. Two parenthesised monitoring readings are supplied per `CLAUDE.md` §4",
