        "body": "- **The lecture covers four:** **antidepressants** · **antipsychotics** · **mood stabilizers** · **benzodiazepines**. **Every one is delivered** — no class is orphaned.\n- **Where pharmacotherapy sits:** the deck opens on a **modalities** slide — **psychotherapy**, **pharmacotherapy**, **neurostimulation (TMS and ECT)**, and a fourth panel the lecturer never labelled. **`ps-therapy` and `ps-devices` are the other two.**\n\n### ⚠️⚠️ NEITHER SOURCE PRINTS A DOSE — and the exception is a single line\n|**Source**|**Quantitative content, exhaustively**|\n|---|---|\n|**`L17`, 31 slides**|**ONE** — benzodiazepines **must NOT be used more than ONE MONTH**. A regex for `mg`, `mmol`, `mEq`, `ng/ml`, `mcg` over the whole cache returns **nothing else**|\n|**Book ch.11, 4 pages**|**ONE numeric value with a unit — lithium `RANGE: 0.6-1.2 mEq/L`.** Everything else is a **time limit** or a **risk figure**|\n\n- **⚠️ Fifteen pages, one unit:** across this book's pharmacology **the lithium range is the ONLY value with a unit anywhere** — book ch.3 prints none either (`ps-anxiety`'s `ax-9`), and book ch.7 prints none for any of seven antipsychotics (`ps-schizmgmt`'s `sm-6`).\n- **⚠️ So every dose — in this chapter is SUPPLIED and tagged**, per `CLAUDE.md` §4. **This is the module's purest fill-and-tag chapter**, and the tag is not optional: **no dose here is the lecturer's.**\n\n### ⚠️ Why the adverse effects get more space than the drugs\n- **The book's own banner**, printed across the foot of its antidepressant poster: ***difficulty tolerating adverse effects is the MOST COMMON REASON for antidepressant discontinuation.*** **That sentence is the argument for the whole of `ph-4` to `ph-6`.**\n\nSrc: L17 sl.2 · sl.4; neuropsychiatry.pdf (ASM MINDS) Psychiatry ch.11 printed pp. 261 and 264. The dose sweeps were run over both caches at the hub; the fifteen-page comparison is against ch.3 (printed 193-201) and ch.7 (printed 228-238)",
