        "h": "Antidepressant adverse effects, class by class",
        "body": "|**Class**|**Adverse effects**|\n|---|---|\n|**TCAs**|**ANTICHOLINERGIC** — peripheral: **dry mouth, constipation, blurring of vision, urine retention**; central: **confusion, memory loss especially in the ELDERLY** · **ANTIHISTAMINIC** — **weight gain, sedation** · **ANTIADRENERGIC** — **postural hypotension, syncope, sexual dysfunction** · **QUINIDINE-LIKE** — **prolonged QTc, arrhythmias**|\n|**SSRIs**|**nausea, GIT upset, headache, insomnia, anxiety at initiation, sexual dysfunction.** ⚠️ **Most of these STOP with continuation of treatment** — the single most useful counselling line in the chapter|\n|**SNRIs**|**GIT upset, ELEVATION OF BLOOD PRESSURE, insomnia, headache, sexual dysfunction**|\n|**NDRI**|**insomnia, decreased appetite, RISK OF SEIZURES.** ⚠️ ***Usually NO sexual dysfunction***|\n|**NaSSAs**|**sedation, increased appetite and weight.** ⚠️ ***Usually NO sexual dysfunction***|\n|**MAOIs**|**⚠️⚠️ NOTHING. See below**|\n\n### The deck's mnemonic — the four S's of SSRI side effects\n- **S**erotonin syndrome · **S**uicidal thoughts · **S**exual dysfunction · **S**tomach upset.\n- **⚠️ It is not the same list as the book's**, and the difference is instructive: the mnemonic carries the **two dangerous** ones, the book's prose carries the **four common** ones. **Learn both.**\n\n### ⚠️⚠️ The MAOI gap — the numbered list runs 1 to 5 and stops\n- **The book gives adverse effects for TCAs — SSRIs, SNRIs, NDRI and NaSSAs, and there is NO `6) MAOIs` entry.** Beyond the poster's own **TYRAMINE CHEESE REACTION** label, MAOIs get nothing.\n- **⚠️ And book ch.7 does not fill it either** — *MAOI*, *monoamine oxidase*, *phenelzine* and *tyramine* **appear on none of its eleven pages** (`ps-schizmgmt`'s `sm-10`). **The gap is real, not a reading failure.**\n- **Supplied, tagged** *(not taken from the course material)*: **hypertensive crisis** with tyramine-rich food — aged cheese, cured meat, fermented foods, red wine — and with sympathomimetics; **serotonin syndrome** if combined with an SSRI or SNRI, requiring a **washout of about two weeks** (**five weeks after fluoxetine**); plus **postural hypotension**, **insomnia** and **weight gain**.\n\n### ⚠️ Two side-effect slides that carry no words\n- **SNRI side effects (sl.11)** is a **title and four uncaptioned photographs** — a woman holding her temples, a blood-pressure cuff, a couple back to back, an unhappy stomach. **The deck names no SNRI side effect in words**; the book's four-item line above is where they come from. ⚠️ **A picture deck is only readable because the book is legible** — and that is the `L17` diagnosis from `theory-plan.md` arriving in practice.\n\nSrc: L17 sl.10 (the four S's, watermarked OSMOSIS.org) · sl.11 (four uncaptioned photographs) · sl.12 (NDRI and NaSSA, the *usually NO sexual dysfunctions* clauses printed in red); neuropsychiatry.pdf (ASM MINDS) Psychiatry ch.11 printed pp. 261-262 — Adverse Effects 1) to 5), which is where the numbering stops. The MAOI block is supplied per `CLAUDE.md` §4",
        "qs": []
