        "id": "ph-5",
        "w": "must",
        "h": "Serotonin syndrome and discontinuation syndrome",
        "body": "### Serotonin syndrome — uncommon, and the 24 hours is the examinable part\n- **Almost any medication involved — with serotonin can cause it**; it appears at **HIGH DOSES or WITH COMBINATIONS**.\n- **⚠️ WITHIN 24 HOURS:** proper medical intervention **decreases mortality a lot**. **The only time-to-treat figure in the chapter.**\n|**Source**|**Manifestations printed**|\n|---|---|\n|**Book**, five|**restlessness · fever · MYOCLONUS · insomnia · confusion**|\n|**Deck**, eleven|**headaches · nausea and diarrhoea · tremors or muscle spasms · rapid heart rate · high blood pressure · disorientation or hallucinations · intense anxiety · high fever · seizures · COMA · DEATH**|\n\n- **⚠️ The deck's list is longer — and reaches further** — it runs to **coma and death**, which is what makes the 24-hour window matter. **The book's five are the early ones; the deck's tail is the reason to act on them.** ⚠️ **Neither source names a single precipitant drug pair**, and neither names **cyproheptadine** or any treatment beyond *proper medical intervention*.\n\n### Discontinuation syndrome — who gets it\n- **On an antidepressant for AT LEAST FOUR WEEKS**, and **recently stopped** — **abruptly or after a FAST TAPER**.\n\n|**Group**|**Symptoms**|\n|---|---|\n|**Flu-like / general somatic**|nausea, vomiting, diarrhoea, headaches, sweating; the deck adds **lethargy, tremor, anorexia**|\n|**Sleep disturbance**|**insomnia, nightmares**, constant sleepiness; the deck adds **excessive dreaming**|\n|**Sensory / movement**|imbalance, tremors, vertigo, dizziness, **ELECTRIC-SHOCK-LIKE experiences**; the deck adds **paraesthesia, numbness, a rushing noise in the head, PALINOPSIA (visual trails)**|\n|**Mood**|dysphoria, anxiety, agitation; the deck adds **irritability, low mood**|\n|**Cognitive**|**confusion and hyper-arousal**|\n|**Disequilibrium** *(deck only, as its own spoke)*|light-headedness, dizziness, vertigo|\n\n- **⚠️ The electric-shock sensation:** the **pathognomonic one**, and both sources print it. *(That it is called a brain zap, and that it is commonest with short-half-life SSRIs such as paroxetine, is supplied.)*\n- **⚠️ ONE SYMPTOM IS ILLEGIBLE:** and it is **not guessed at**. The deck's **fourth Affective symptom** is covered by a **Google Translate floating button baked into the lecturer's screenshot**; only **`…ess`** survives at 600 dpi. **Not inferred** — the obstruction is in the raster, so no re-render recovers it.\n\nSrc: L17 sl.13 (the eleven-item serotonin syndrome image) · sl.14 (the six-spoke discontinuation figure, and the illegible item); neuropsychiatry.pdf (ASM MINDS) Psychiatry ch.11 printed p.262 — *Other adverse effects & special considerations* 1) and 6), and *Common symptoms include*. The two parenthesised readings are supplied per `CLAUDE.md` §4",
