        "h": "Mood stabilizers — and the one number in the chapter",
        "body": "- **Four indications, both sources identical:** **bipolar disorder · recurrent major depressive disorder · borderline personality disorder · schizoaffective disorder.**\n- **⚠️ Only one of the four is bipolar.** A mood stabilizer in a **borderline** or **schizoaffective** patient is on-label here.\n\n|**Group**|**Agents**|\n|---|---|\n|**Lithium**|lithium|\n|**Anticonvulsants**|**carbamazepine · lamotrigine · valproic acid / divalproex** *(the book writes **sodium valproate**)*|\n|**Atypical antipsychotics**|**quetiapine · olanzapine · lurasidone · aripiprazole · risperidone · paliperidone** — with a **MOOD-STABILIZING EFFECT even WITHOUT psychosis**|\n\n- **⚠️ The book's list is shorter:** only **FOUR of those six** — it omits **lurasidone** and **paliperidone**, which appear in the deck alone. **And all six appear here only as mood stabilizers**, never in `ph-7`'s antipsychotic table.\n\n### Lithium — ⚠️ `RANGE: 0.6-1.2 mEq/L`, and it is printed in the BOOK ONLY\n|**Grouping**|**Effects**|\n|---|---|\n|**TOXICITY** *(book)*|**polyuria · polydipsia · nausea · diarrhoea · ATAXIA**|\n|**COMMON** *(book)*|**lethargy · weight gain**|\n|**LESS COMMON** *(book)*|blurred vision · tremors · mildly ill|\n|**⚠️ CONTRAINDICATED**|**in PREGNANCY and LACTATION**|\n\n- **The deck's LITHIUM mnemonic:** **L**eukocytosis · **I**ncreased urine output (polyuria) · **T**remors and **T**hirst (polydipsia) · **H**ypothyroidism · **I**ncreased weight gain · **U**pset stomach · **M**uscle weakness and **M**ental issues. ⚠️ **Leukocytosis and hypothyroidism are the deck's alone** — the book's three groups name neither.\n- **Acute toxicity, the deck's CAN HAM SUCS:** **C**onfusion · **A**n increase in urine and thirst · **N**ausea · **H**and tremors · **A**taxia · **M**uscle twitches · **S**eizures · **U**ncontrollable eye movements · **C**oma · **S**lurred speech.\n- **⚠️ The deck gives NO toxic threshold** and the book gives the therapeutic range without one. **The two halves of the same number are in different sources**, and **neither prints the level at which CAN HAM SUCS begins.** *(Above about 1.5 mEq/L — supplied.)*\n\n### Anticonvulsants — monitoring, and both sources agree almost word for word\n|**Drug**|**Adverse effects**|**⚠️ Monitor**|\n|---|---|---|\n|**Sodium valproate**|**stomach irritation · weight gain · tremors · menstrual irregularities · POLYCYSTIC OVARY**. ⚠️ **Contraindicated in pregnancy and lactation**|**LIVER FUNCTION and CBC**|\n|**Lamotrigine**|used for **BIPOLAR DEPRESSION** (maintenance phase)|**⚠️ STEVENS-JOHNSON SYNDROME** — *a very rare but potentially fatal skin condition* — **highly important**|\n|**Carbamazepine**|**can LOWER the white blood cell count**|**CBC**|\n\n- **⚠️ Carbamazepine has no prose block — in the book.** It appears in the **poster** as `CBC → LOWER WHITE BLOOD CELL COUNT` and is **absent from the book's own *Notes not included in the illustration* table**, which covers valproate, lamotrigine and benzodiazepines only. **The deck's slide 26 is where it is written in words.**\n- **⚠️ Two of the three are monitored by CBC — and for opposite reasons** — valproate **with** liver function, carbamazepine **for the white count**. **Lamotrigine is monitored by looking at the skin, not by a test.**\n\nSrc: L17 sl.22 (indications — ⚠️ the `Indications:` line exists in the text layer but is COVERED ON SCREEN by the banner image stacked over it, and was recovered from `pdftotext`) · sl.23 · sl.24 (LITHIUM) · sl.25 (CAN HAM SUCS) · sl.26; neuropsychiatry.pdf (ASM MINDS) Psychiatry ch.11 printed p.264 — the landscape MOOD STABILIZERS poster read at 300 dpi and the *Notes not included in the illustration* table. The toxic threshold is supplied per `CLAUDE.md` §4",
