        "h": "Sciatica — a symptom, not a diagnosis",
        "body": "- **⚠️ The book says it twice over:** sciatica is **the most frequent RADICULAR PAIN syndrome of spinal origin**, and it is **A SYMPTOM, NOT A DIAGNOSIS.**\n- **What it describes:** pain **referred down the course of the SCIATIC NERVE** — from the back to the buttock, down the back of the leg to the foot; beginning in the lower back and radiating to the **sacroiliac regions, buttocks, thighs, calf and foot**.\n- **Where it comes from:** irritation of a spinal nerve root with disc herniation at **L4-L5 OR L5-S1**, most often compromising the **L5 and S1 roots**.\n\n|**Cause**|**Detail**|\n|---|---|\n|**Inflammatory**|**sciatic neuritis** · **arachnoiditis**|\n|**Nerve root compression — in the VERTEBRAL CANAL**|lumbar disc prolapse · tumour · **TB**|\n|**— in the INTERVERTEBRAL FORAMEN**|**root canal stenosis** from osteoarthritis, **spondylolisthesis**, facet arthropathy, tumours|\n|**— in the BUTTOCK or PELVIS**|abscess · tumours · haematoma|\n|**PIRIFORMIS SYNDROME** *(wallet sciatica / fat wallet syndrome)*|the sciatic nerve is **compressed or irritated by the PIRIFORMIS MUSCLE**, giving pain, tingling and numbness in the buttocks and along the nerve. **Caused or aggravated by sitting with a large wallet in the affected side's rear pocket**|\n|**Diabetic ischaemia**|of the nerve and roots|\n|**Neoplastic involvement**|—|\n\n### Clinical picture\n- **Onset is often TRAUMATIC** — exertion or a forced movement gives acute low back pain, then referral to the leg.\n- **Exacerbated by standing — sitting, exertion, coughing and sneezing. RELIEVED BY LYING DOWN.**\n- **Distally:** the pain **may be REPLACED by tingling or numbness.**\n\n|**Topography**|**As printed on 161**|\n|---|---|\n|**L5**|buttock, **ANTERIOR aspect of thigh**, lateral malleolus, **dorsum of foot**, great toe or the medial three toes|\n|**S1**|buttock, **posterior aspect of thigh**, knee, leg and heel, to the **sole or lateral side of the foot** up to the fifth toe|\n\n- **⚠️⚠️ A contradiction about L5:** here the chapter gives L5 the **anterior** thigh; **the root table in `bp-7` gives L5 the BACK of the leg** and assigns the **anterior thigh to L3** and the **anteromedial shin to L4**. **Both are recorded and neither is reconciled** — but the table's version is the one that agrees with the dermatome figure printed beside it.\n\n### Two clinical forms\n|**Form**|**Features**|\n|---|---|\n|**HYPERALGIC sciatica**|**severe pain**; the patient **prefers to remain in bed** and is **hesitant even to move slightly**|\n|**— its specific form, MYALGIC sciatica**|commonest in **S1** herniations. Neuralgic pain **with intense, often continuous muscular pain and cramps** in **biceps femoris, triceps surae** and occasionally the gluteals. **Mild motor deficit**|\n|**PARALYTIC sciatica**|**slight motor deficit** detectable; more frequent in **L5**. **Paralytic L5 sciatica most often leads to FOOT DROP**, forcing the patient to modify the gait|\n\n- **⚠️ The two forms split — on which root and which deficit.** **Hyperalgic/myalgic is S1 and pain-dominant; paralytic is L5 and weakness-dominant.** **Foot drop names the root.**\n\nSrc: neuropsychiatry.pdf (ASM MINDS) Neurology ch.16 printed pp. 160-161 — `2. Sciatica`, Definition, Causes, Clinical Picture with the L5/S1 topography, and Clinical Forms of Sciatica; the L5 contradiction is against the root table on printed p.164. L11 sl. Sciatica prints the definition, three of the causes, and *sciatic neuropathy is a clinical diagnosis*",
