        "w": "must",
        "h": "What else it could be, and what to image with",
        "body": "|**Differential**|**How it declares itself**|\n|---|---|\n|**SPONDYLOARTHROPATHY**|**usually YOUNG**; pain **does NOT refer distal to the knee**; **bilateral or alternating**, episodic; **not modified by activity**; **NOCTURNAL pain common**; **ESR ELEVATED**; **rapid response to medication**. Diagnosis: **PA views of pelvis** or specialized **sacroiliac** hip views|\n|**INTRAMEDULLARY TUMOURS (gliomas)**|**NOCTURNAL pain common**; the patient **stands or walks to get relief**; **physical activity has NO influence**; spine sometimes **very stiff**; **radiographs NORMAL**. Diagnosis: **MRI / CT / myelography**. **Surgery relieves the patient**|\n|**METASTASES or MULTIPLE MYELOMA**|**intense refractory sciatic pain**|\n|**Infectious discitis**|⚠️ **named, no clause at all**|\n|**Infectious sacroiliitis**|⚠️ **named, no clause at all**|\n|**Pseudosciatic syndromes**|the book's only example: **periarthritis of the hip** *(printed `Psuedosciatic` [sic])*|\n\n- **⚠️ Nocturnal pain appears — in BOTH of the two expanded differentials — and it is red flag 4** (`bp-1`). **It is the single most transferable finding on this page.**\n- **⚠️ Movement separates them:** spondyloarthropathy is **not modified by activity** but responds fast to medication; a glioma's pain is **relieved by standing or walking** and has **normal radiographs**. **Normal films with night pain is the combination that must not be reassured.**\n- **⚠️ Three of the six:** **named and never expanded**, two with **no clause whatsoever**. **A list is not coverage.**\n\n### Imaging and diagnosis\n|**Modality**|**What the chapter says**|\n|---|---|\n|**Radiography**|**normal on most occasions**; may show **loss of lumbar lordosis**, scoliosis, **reduced intervertebral disc space**|\n|**CT**|morphologic abnormalities of a herniated disc; impact on adjacent soft tissue; **neuroforaminal or extraforaminal encroachment**|\n|**Myelography**|**excellent for the entire subarachnoid space**; assesses **spinal stenosis**. ⚠️ **Disadvantages: headache, nausea**|\n|**Discography**|**an often neglected modality**; excellent for **disc pathology**|\n|**MRI**|**⚠️ STUDY OF CHOICE — but SCOPED**: *for recurrence following discectomy, to differentiate recurrent herniation from perineural fibrosis*|\n\n- **⚠️⚠️ Read that MRI line carefully.** It is **not** a general statement that MRI is best for back pain; the chapter's general imaging advice is in `bp-8` — **no imaging for acute mechanical LBP without red flags**. ⚠️ **And the cervical half of the same chapter prints `MRI of cervical spine: the best diagnostic technique` UNSCOPED** (`nr-neckpain`). **The chapter is stricter about the lumbar spine than the cervical one, and does not say why.**\n- **⚠️ A clinical diagnosis:** **sciatic neuropathy** is one, and the deck prints it too. **Every modality above is for the cause, not the syndrome.**\n\nSrc: neuropsychiatry.pdf (ASM MINDS) Neurology ch.16 printed p.162 — Differential Diagnosis and `IMAGING & Diagnosis`; the cervical MRI comparison is printed p.171. L11 sl. Sciatica prints the clinical-diagnosis line",
