        "h": "Cauda equina syndrome — the surgical emergency",
        "body": "- **What it is:** a **LARGE CENTRAL lumbar disc herniation** compressing the cauda equina.\n- **⚠️⚠️ It NEEDS EARLY SURGICAL INTERVENTION — any delay leads to PERMANENT neurological deficit.** It is the one **absolute** surgical indication in `bp-8` that is not a failure of something else.\n\n|**Domain**|**Findings**|\n|---|---|\n|**MOTOR**|**BILATERAL LMN weakness of dorsiflexion and plantarflexion — FLAIL FOOT**. Hip flexors/extensors and knee flexors/extensors may also weaken|\n|**SENSORY**|**bilateral hypoaesthesia** by the roots affected, with **SADDLE-SHAPED hypoaesthesia** from the **sacral** roots|\n|**REFLEXES**|**ABSENT ANKLE AND KNEE REFLEXES, bilaterally**|\n|**SPHINCTERIC (LMN)**|**URINE RETENTION with overflow incontinence**; **chronic constipation**|\n|**AUTONOMIC**|**sexual dysfunction** — impotence in males|\n|**SLRT**|**POSITIVE BILATERALLY**|\n\n- **⚠️ Everything here is BILATERAL — and that is the whole diagnosis.** Unilateral leg signs are `bp-3`'s radiculopathy; **bilateral signs with a saddle and a bladder are this**. **Both sources print the word *bilateral* on four separate lines.**\n- **⚠️ It is LOWER motor neurone throughout** — flaccid bladder, absent reflexes, flail foot. **The cauda equina is roots, not cord**, which is why nothing here is spastic.\n\n### Which root, and how often\n|**Disc**|**Root**|**Frequency**|\n|---|---|---|\n|**L5-S1**|**S1**|**the most common**|\n|**L4-L5**|**L5**|next|\n|**L3-L4**|**L4**|**to a lesser extent**|\n\n- **⚠️ Reading the root off the disc:** it is **one number BELOW the lower vertebra** here — L5-S1 hits S1, L4-L5 hits L5. **Read the disc name and the root falls out of it.**\n\nSrc: neuropsychiatry.pdf (ASM MINDS) Neurology ch.16 printed pp. 163-164 — `3. Cauda equina syndrome.` and its Clinical picture, which runs across the page break; L11 sl. Cauda equina syndrome · Symptoms, which print the same findings and the same root frequencies",
