        "h": "Localising the lumbar and sacral roots",
        "body": "|**Root**|**Pain**|**Dermatome**|**Muscle**|**Reflex**|\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n|**L3**|**front of the thigh**|anterior thigh|**quadriceps, adductors**|**⚠️ *(blank on the page)***|\n|**L4**|front of thigh, **knee**, and **medial shin**|**anteromedial shin**|quadriceps, **tibialis anterior**, hamstrings|**KNEE JERK**|\n|**L5**|**back of leg**, lateral lower leg, **dorsum of foot to great toe**|dorsum of foot to great toe|**extensor hallucis longus**, **evertors**, hamstring|**⚠️ *(blank on the page)***|\n|**S1**|**sole of foot**, lateral side of foot, back of thigh and leg|**sole**|**plantar flexors**, toe flexors, hamstrings|**ANKLE JERK**|\n|**Lower sacral**|**buttocks, saddle area**|saddle area, perineal|**anal muscles**|**ANAL**|\n\n- **⚠️⚠️ Two blank reflex cells:** **L3 and L5**, on the page. **Verified at 300 dpi: they are not merged cells and not illegible — the book prints no reflex for those two roots.** ⚠️ **That is not an error to correct.** **L5 genuinely has no routinely tested deep tendon reflex**, which is exactly why `bp-4`'s paralytic L5 sciatica declares itself as **foot drop** rather than as a lost jerk. *(That reading is supplied; the book does not explain the blanks.)*\n- **⚠️ The two reflexes — that ARE printed are the two that localise:** **knee jerk = L4**, **ankle jerk = S1**. Together with the **anal reflex** for the lower sacral roots they are the whole reflex content of the table.\n- **⚠️ Read this table against `bp-4`.** Its **L5 pain is the BACK of the leg**; `bp-4`'s topography gives L5 the **anterior** thigh, which this table assigns to **L3**. **The contradiction is the chapter's own.** The **dermatome figure printed beside this table** — lower limbs banded L1 to S5 — **agrees with the table.**\n- **⚠️ Only two roots share a muscle group.** **Quadriceps is L3 and L4**; everything else is distinct, so **a weak quadriceps needs the reflex and the dermatome to localise, and a weak EHL does not.**\n\nSrc: neuropsychiatry.pdf (ASM MINDS) Neurology ch.16 printed p.164 — `Localizing features of lumbar and sacral root lesions`, the five-row table re-read at 300 dpi to confirm the two empty Reflex cells, and the unnumbered *Lower limb dermatomes* figure beneath it. The reading of why L5 has no reflex is supplied per `CLAUDE.md` §4",
