        "h": "Localising the cervical roots",
        "body": "|**Modality**|**C5**|**C6**|**C7**|**C8**|\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n|**Sensory**|**lateral arm**|**THUMB**|**MIDDLE FINGER**|**LITTLE FINGER**|\n|**Motor**|**deltoid**|**wrist extensors**|**triceps**|**finger flexion**|\n|**Disc**|**C4-C5**|**C5-C6**|**C6-C7**|**C7-T1**|\n|**Reflex**|**BICEPS**|**BRACHIORADIALIS**|**TRICEPS**|**hand muscles**|\n\n- **⚠️ Every cervical root HAS a reflex** — and in the lumbar table two cells are blank (`bp-7`). **That asymmetry is the single most useful difference between the two tables.**\n- **⚠️ The fingers name the root:** **thumb = C6**, **middle finger = C7**, **little finger = C8**. **The deck's second question is exactly this row** (`np-8`).\n- **⚠️ THE TABLE HAS NO T1 COLUMN**, although the figure printed directly beneath it teaches T1 — **interossei, and the dermatome on the medial upper arm**. **`np-1`'s weak little-finger abduction has no home in this table.**\n- **⚠️ The two root tables are TRANSPOSED:** the lumbar one (`bp-7`) puts **roots in the rows**; this one puts **roots in the columns**. **Same book, same chapter, five pages apart.** Read the axis before reading the cell.\n\n### The five-root panel printed beneath it\n|**Root**|**Motor, as the panel labels it**|**Sensation**|\n|---|---|---|\n|**C5**|**deltoid**, **biceps**|the dermatome **over the deltoid**|\n|**C6**|**biceps**, **wrist extensors** — *extensor carpi radialis longus and brevis*|**lateral forearm and hand**|\n|**C7**|**triceps**, **wrist flexors**, **finger extensors**|the small dermatome over the **middle finger**|\n|**C8**|**finger flexors**|**medial hand and forearm**|\n|**T1**|**INTEROSSEI** — the intrinsic muscles of the hand|**medial upper arm**|\n\n- **⚠️ The panel — and the table disagree about C5 and C6 motor.** The panel gives **biceps to BOTH C5 and C6**; the table gives C5 **deltoid** and C6 **wrist extensors** only. **Both recorded** — and the panel is the more standard reading, since biceps is a C5-C6 muscle. *(That judgement is supplied.)*\n\nSrc: neuropsychiatry.pdf (ASM MINDS) Neurology ch.16 printed p.169 — `Localizing features of cervical root lesion`, the unnumbered upper-extremity dermatome figures, and the five-row root panel beneath them, all read as printed",
