        "id": "np-7",
        "w": "must",
        "h": "Investigations and treatment of neck pain",
        "body": "### Investigations\n|**Type**|**What is printed**|\n|---|---|\n|**Laboratory**|**CBC, ESR, Hb, CRP, RA & MT** ⚠️ *(`RA` and `MT` are abbreviations the chapter never expands — recorded, not decoded)*|\n|**Plain X-ray of cervical spine**|**loss of normal cervical lordosis** · **narrowing of the disc space** · **osteophytes** · **cervical subluxation**|\n|**— AP view**|the **lateral masses**, equal separation of spinous processes, **best view for VERTICAL COMPRESSION FRACTURES**, equal disc spaces|\n|**— Lateral view**|each cervical vertebra, cartilaginous disc space, **cervical lordotic curve**, C2 neural arch, lamina, spinous process, lateral mass, articular facet|\n|**— Swimmer's view**|**if the body of C7 is not well visualized on the lateral view**|\n|**— Odontoid view**|**visualize the dens**|\n|**⚠️ MRI of cervical spine**|**THE BEST DIAGNOSTIC TECHNIQUE**|\n|**CT cervical spine**|more useful for **BONY DETAILS** — osteophytes, **congenital cervical stenosis**, and **OPLL** (ossified posterior longitudinal ligament)|\n\n- **⚠️⚠️ MRI — unscoped here, scoped there:** `nr-backpain`'s `bp-5` prints MRI as **study of choice *for recurrence following discectomy***; this page prints it flatly as **the best diagnostic technique**. **The chapter is stricter about the lumbar spine than the cervical and does not say why. Both recorded, neither reconciled.**\n- **⚠️ One view, one question:** **AP for compression fractures**, **lateral for alignment**, **swimmer's when C7 is hidden**, **odontoid for the dens**. **The lateral view is the one that fails at C7**, which is exactly what the swimmer's view exists for.\n\n### Treatment by onset\n|**Onset**|**What is printed**|\n|---|---|\n|**ACUTE**|occurs **suddenly from an injury or stress**. **Most neck pain RESOLVES ITSELF WITHIN 7-10 DAYS** with **rest, ice and over-the-counter pain relievers**. If symptoms **persist longer than a few weeks**, **conservative therapy** — anti-inflammatory medication, **injections**, physical therapy, **acupuncture**, **chiropractic care**|\n|**CHRONIC**|**neck pain lasting longer than 3 MONTHS**. Those who found no relief conservatively **may benefit from pain management by PHYSICAL THERAPY or SURGICAL intervention**|\n\n- **The governing sentence:** treatment **depends on aetiology and onset**; the **main treatment is to treat the underlying causative factor**, and **sometimes emergency first aid is necessary for traumatic neck pain**.\n- **⚠️ 3 months is the chronic threshold — in BOTH halves of the chapter** — the same figure `nr-backpain`'s `bp-2` gives for chronic back pain.\n\n### Management modalities\n- **Conservative — REST:** important for **all acute neck pain**, alongside other remedies. **Duration depends on prognosis and causative factors**, with **proper positioning of the neck** as directed, and **avoidance of heavy lifting**.\n- **Drugs:** **analgesic, anti-inflammatory, injections** ⚠️ *(no agent and no dose is printed)*.\n- **Physical therapy:** **ICE in the acute stage** or cryotherapy, **followed by moist heat**, contrast therapy and hot compression · **exercise therapy** — neck muscle **strengthening**, **mobilizing**, **manipulation technique**, **cervical traction** · **orthotic devices** — **cervical collar**, **halter collar**, **halo cervical traction**, **contour pillows**.\n- **ADL — activities of daily living, for correction of life:** **regular exercise**, **proper lying, sitting and working**, **proper body alignment**, appropriate **ERGONOMICS**.\n\n### Surgery\n|**⚠️ Indications**|**Procedures**|\n|---|---|\n|**BRACHIALGIA not responding to medical treatment** · **PROGRESSIVE NEUROLOGICAL DEFICIT due to root compression** · **manifestations of CORD COMPRESSION, i.e. MYELOPATHY**|**DISCECTOMY** (with or without fusion) — removes herniated material pressing on a root or the cord · **CERVICAL SPINAL FUSION** · **SPINAL DECOMPRESSION** — removing part of a bone or disc · **artificial disc replacement** · **percutaneous radiofrequency neurotomy**|\n\n- **✅ The deck prints the same three indications**, in the same order.\n- **⚠️ `Post-Operative Physiotherapy`:** a **heading with no body**, and **the last line of the chapter**. Nothing follows it; the page ends there.\n- **⚠️ `pain management` is named twice — and never taught**, which is the second time this chapter promises pain management and does not deliver it — see `nr-backpain`'s `bp-9`.\n\nSrc: neuropsychiatry.pdf (ASM MINDS) Neurology ch.16 printed pp. 171-172 — `Investigations for neck pain:`, `Treatment of Neck Pain According to Onset:`, `Management Modalities:` and the surgery block, to the end of the chapter; L11 sl. Investigations · Treatment, which print the plain film, MRI and CT lines and the same three surgical indications",
